  "Number": 11,
  "Question": "A running track is the ring formed by two concentric circles. It is \\(10\\) feet wide. The circumferences of the two circles differ by about:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(10\\) feet",
    "B": "\\(30\\) feet",
    "C": "\\(60\\) feet",
    "D": "\\(100\\) feet",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The track's width is the difference between the two radii. Let the inner radius be \\(r>0\\) feet, so the outer radius is \\(r+10\\) feet. Using \\(C=2\\pi r\\), the circumference difference is\n\\[\n2\\pi(r+10)-2\\pi r=20\\pi\\text{ feet}.\n\\]\nThe unknown inner radius cancels, so the width alone determines the difference. Since \\(20\\pi\\approx62.8\\), the best listed approximation is 60 feet, choice C.",
